From: =?utf-8?b?T3R0byBLZWvDpGzDpGluZW4=?= <otto@debian.org>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: Make 'watch and exec a program that is overwritten' test race-free

execve(2) fails with ETXTBSY ('Text file busy') when the file being
executed is still open for writing by another process. The test
overwrote the watched script in place with 'cat >', so when entr reacts
quickly to the change it may attempt to re-exec the script while cat
still holds the write file descriptor open. This is timing dependent
and intermittently fails on loaded or slower CI runners (observed on
Debian arm64 autopkgtests).

Instead write the new content to a temporary file and atomically
replace the watched file with mv(1). entr handles replaced files
(IN_MOVE_SELF mapped to NOTE_RENAME) and re-executes the new content,
and the new inode is never open for writing, so the race is eliminated.
The replacement file is explicitly chmod'ed executable, as the mode of
the file being replaced is not inherited over a rename.

Forwarded: no, should be
---
 system_test.sh | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/system_test.sh b/system_test.sh
index 9de887c..96ebd04 100755
--- a/system_test.sh
+++ b/system_test.sh
@@ -566,10 +566,16 @@ try "watch and exec a program that is overwritten"
 	touch $tmp/script; chmod 755 $tmp/script
 	echo $tmp/script | entr -p $tmp/script $tmp/file1 > $tmp/exec.out &
 	bgpid=$! ; zz
-	cat > $tmp/script <<-EOF
+	# Write the new content to a temporary file and atomically replace the
+	# watched script with mv(1). Overwriting the file in place with 'cat >'
+	# races with entr re-executing it: execve(2) fails with ETXTBSY when the
+	# file is still open for writing by another process.
+	cat > $tmp/script.new <<-EOF
 	#!/bin/sh
 	echo vroom
 	EOF
+	chmod 755 $tmp/script.new
+	mv $tmp/script.new $tmp/script
 	zz ; kill -INT $bgpid
 	wait $bgpid; assert "$?" "0"
 	assert "$(cat $tmp/exec.out)" "vroom"
